// expect-error: NITPICK-REACH-002
// expect-error-at: 44:5
//
// THE FIRST REJECTION FIXTURE, and it is the consumer rather than a language
// probe on purpose. `tests/probe/refused/` holds seven refusals about the
// LANGUAGE; this directory holds refusals about USING THIS LIBRARY, which is
// the thing a consumer can break.
//
// WHAT IT PINS. A program that imports `nregex` and drops a system arm from its
// `failsafe` is refused by name: `(*)` discharges exhaustiveness (PICK-003) and
// nothing else, and REACH-002 says so in as many words — "`(*)` counts for
// nothing here". Run by hand at cycle 0.0.1 as control E6
// (`../conformance/TRANSCRIPT.txt`); committed at 0.0.3 so it is re-run rather
// than remembered.
//
// AND WHY IT IS THE RIGHT FIXTURE FOR RULE B-7. The import below is RELATIVE
// (B-15, O-G3). If it is ever typo'd or the file moves, `npkc` exits 1 with
// `NITPICK-RESOLVE-005` — a genuine refusal, and this test wanted a refusal —
// so without code-set EQUALITY this file would pass having refused the PATH
// instead of the missing arm. That is the hazard measured in
// `../conformance/TRANSCRIPT.txt` §G, and this fixture is the standing
// instance of it rather than a constructed one.
//
// NOT THIS, and it is worth writing down because it was the first idea: a
// consumer missing the `(ERegexPattern)` arm still COMPILES. Control E4
// measured it — nothing in the program can raise one while the public surface
// has no callable entry point, so REACH-002 does not demand the arm yet. The
// day `regex_compile` exists, that changes, and this comment is where a reader
// looks to find out why there is no such fixture here.
mod:failsafe_missing_system_arm;

use "../../src/lib.npk".*;

func:main = int32(int32:argc, cstring[]:argv) {
    discard(argc);
    discard(argv);
    exit 0i32;
};

// `(WildLeak)` is the arm that is missing. Everything else is `import.npk`'s
// `failsafe` verbatim, so the diff between the two files is the one line the
// diagnostic is about.
func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (ERegexPattern)  { exit 60i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
